WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.040
Pack it, cost the whole day, and sell the batch.

2
00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:08.080
This is the video for module 4 of Basic Baking and Snacks Production.

3
00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:22.470
The most common mistake is packing too soon. Warm snacks release steam, the crust goes soft, and mould grows faster. Fried snacks usually need about 30 minutes on a rack, and loaves an hour or more.

4
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.050
Sealed warm, the sugar on doughnuts melts and goes sticky.

5
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.350
Bread bagged warm gets a wet crust and early mould.

6
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:37.190
Coconut candy goes sticky in humid air, so wrap it and keep it airtight.

7
00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:51.810
Fish rolls are the exception. Their filling is perishable, so cool them just enough, keep them covered and get them to the customer quickly. Use only food-grade packs, never newspaper or old chemical sacks.

8
00:00:53.060 --> 00:01:03.750
Every pack needs these six things, with allergens clearly highlighted. Set your best before date from your own keeping test, a little shorter than the day the product first went off.

9
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.070
Never print a NAFDAC number you have not been issued.

10
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:13.930
Many bakers copy the neighbour's price and wonder where the money went. Cost the whole day instead.

11
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:23.620
For example, Bisi makes 48 buns and 30 doughnuts, and keeps 2 of each for testing. So she can sell 46 buns and 28 doughnuts.

12
00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:29.420
Ingredients for the four batches cost 8,128 naira.

13
00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:41.120
Then the costs people forget: oil, gas, bags, labels, delivery, and her own 6 hours of labour. Together, 13,290 naira.

14
00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:50.310
The whole day costs 21,418 naira. These are example prices, so always use your own receipts.

15
00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:58.770
Divide the shared costs by the 74 pieces she can sell. That is about 180 naira per piece.

16
00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:10.850
Add the ingredients, and a bun costs her about 272 naira. She prices it at 350. Copying a neighbour's 200 naira would have lost money.

17
00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:16.630
A doughnut costs about 318 naira, so she charges 400.

18
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:31.340
If all sell, she takes 27,300 naira, and makes 5,882 naira profit after paying herself. Update your costing whenever flour, oil or gas prices change.

19
00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:39.410
Set up WhatsApp Business with a catalogue: photo, price, piece count and allergen note for each product.

20
00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:48.220
Choose a cut-off time for orders, such as 6:00 pm the evening before, and repeat it in every reply.

21
00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:55.550
Write every order in one place: name, products, time, address, amount and payment status.

22
00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.960
For large orders, ask for part payment before you buy ingredients.

23
00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:09.330
Then add up the orders to set your batches, plus a few extra pieces for walk-in buyers, based on your sales record.

24
00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:20.460
Fake transfer alerts are a common scam. Before you hand over goods, confirm the money has arrived in your own account app, however urgent the customer seems.

25
00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:30.220
Do not hold fish rolls over to sell the next day unless they were chilled promptly and you are sure they are safe. When in doubt, throw them away.

26
00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:44.400
Never sell anything mouldy or stale, and never re-label old stock with a new date. Bread still within its tested date can be sold as yesterday's bake, at a discount, kept apart from fresh stock.

27
00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:57.340
To remember. Cool before you pack. Label honestly. Cost the whole day and pay yourself. Confirm every payment. And keep a daily record of pieces made, sold and left over.

28
00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:05.130
For example, Zainab in Abuja checked four weeks of records before she cut Monday production and added a Friday batch.

29
00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:19.550
Now practise. Pack and label one product two ways and test how it keeps. Cost your own production day from real receipts. Then set up your WhatsApp catalogue, order policy and daily sales record.

30
00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:21.840
Continue with the lessons below.
